\f0\fs21\fsmilli10909 \cf2 2. Platform-based surveys (e.g., Prolific, MTurk, convenience pools) can generate measurement\
error in ways that differ from traditional survey modes. Identify two plausible sources of\
measurement error or bias in platform data and explain how you would detect each issue\
using diagnostics in the survey export (e.g., duration, attention checks, missingness patterns,\
straightlining, duplicate IDs).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Two plausible sources of measurement error in platform-based surveys are speeding and duplicate or repeated participation. Speeding occurs when respondents rush through the survey to maximize their hourly earnings, submitting answers without carefully reading the questions. This is more prevalent on platforms like MTurk and Prolific than in traditional phone or in-person surveys because compensation is per-task, creating a direct incentive to finish quickly. To detect speeding, researchers can examine the duration variable in the survey export and flag respondents whose completion time falls well below a plausible reading threshold. For example, less than one-third of the median. Attention check items embedded in the survey provide a second layer of detection: respondents who fail these likely did not engage with the surrounding items either.\
Duplicate participation occurs when the same individual completes the survey more than once, either by using multiple accounts or by accidentally restarting a dropped session \'97 as can happen when a respondent loses their connection and re-enters the survey. This inflates the sample size with non-independent observations and can bias estimates if the duplicated respondent is systematically different from the rest of the sample. To detect this, researchers can check the survey export for duplicate IP addresses, duplicate worker IDs, or suspiciously similar response patterns across entries. Platforms like Prolific track unique participant IDs, so flagging repeated IDs is straightforward; for less structured convenience samples, checking for near-identical response vectors or matching open-ended text across submissions can catch duplicates that evade ID-level checks.\
